  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nA cold front was located from the Mid Atlantic to the Lower \nOhio Valley. A shortwave tracking along the northern side of \nthe front will continue to bring rain to areas south of I-80 \nearly this morning, though these chances will decrease from N-S\nas the wave gradually exits and the front drops slowly south. \nAreas south of the I-68 corridor will be the last to see the \nrain end late today. Highs are expected to be 5 to 10 degrees \nbelow average.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA trough is expected to advance eastward from the Central CONUS\non Friday. Increasing moisture and ascent in warm advection in \nsouthwest flow ahead of the trough, and a surface warm front \nlifting northward, will result in rain chances increasing from \nS-N Friday. Widespread rain is expected Friday night into \nSaturday as the warm front continues lifting north across the \nregion. Precipitable water is progged to increase to between \n1.5 and 1.75 by Saturday morning, with continued warm, moist \nadvection. There is some indication of very limited elevated \ninstability, with the thunder potential low. The warm front \nshould be north of the region by Saturday night, with showers \nand possible thunderstorms as the upper trough axis crosses the\nregion. Current NBM probabilities indicate a 60-80 percent \nchance of at least one inch of rain from Friday through early \nSunday morning, with a 30-50 percent chance of 2 inches. \nProbabilities are lower on the lee side of the ridges, where \nsome low level downsloping is likely. Localized flooding will \nbe possible in flood prone areas, and where heavier rain \npersists.\n\nThe downsloping should result in gusty SE wind especially on \nthe lee side of the Laurel Highland ridges, developing Friday \nand continuing Saturday. At this time, it looks as though \ninversion heights should keep gusts in the 30-35 kt range, \nthough these gusts could be persistent through this period. \nGusts should diminish Saturday night as the flow veers to the \nsouth and low level wind diminishes. \n\nWhile shower and thunderstorm chances continue Sunday, they \nshould be more limited and not an all day rain with the area in\nbetween the exiting shortwave in the morning, and an \napproaching shortwave across the Midwest. The Midwestern \nshortwave/surface trough should track across the region Sunday \nnight, with higher shower/storm chances. More uncertainty \nexists for Monday. Precip chances will depend on where the \nsurface trough stalls across the lower Ohio Valley region. \nMaintained the NBM POPs with a variety of model ensemble \nsolutions and uncertainty. \n\nEnsemble river forecasts indicate elevated river levels are \nlikely beginning over the weekend into next week.",
